hi I'm Marc Horowitz from Yale School of  Medicine in HHMI and I want to tell you  a story about a discovery that has led  to our really wonderful  what's now 15 to 20 year experience of  experimentation that followed this  discovery was really somewhat  serendipitous and I think there was a  lot of luck involved  it was helped enormous ly by two  wonderful collaborators who have  remained very close friends and constant  contacts Ming Chang who's now in Taiwan  and aurash hartal who's now in Martin's  read and so I'll just tell you the story  as it occurred so the story is about  information transfer in the cell as we  know DNA is transcribed into RNA is in  turn translated into protein and the the  step that's involved here is the final  step of information transfer namely that  of protein folding so we know about  machines that carry out transcription  and translation and have very fine  molecular details to those reactions for  some years now increasingly fine but  protein folding has remained somewhat  mysterious and it's really only during  the last decade or two that we've really  started to gain some understanding about  how this works in cells so for those  unfamiliar I just point out that  proteins are composed of 20 different  amino acids and these strings of amino  acids ultimately fold into  characteristic 3-dimensional structures  they carry out biological activities for  example they could be enzymes  cytoskeleton hormones hemoglobin  channels receptors what-have-you  proteins are major effectors in the cell  and so their proper folding is crucial  to them being biologically active  because when they're as effectively  strands of spaghetti leaving a ribosome  they don't necessarily have any  biological activity at that  they have to be folded correctly in  order to be active and so the granddaddy  experiment of protein folding that I  heard about when I was an undergraduate  at Brown University in 1972 I was  carried out by Christian anfinsen in  fact in the late 50s he received his  Nobel Prize Oh Bell Prize for this work  in 1972 a really astonishing experiment  where he started with an enzyme  ribonuclease a hundred odd amino acids  with four disulfide bonds and unfolded  that protein by treating it with  denature and urea and reductant  mercaptoethanol that produced  essentially a random coil version of the  protein and he asked when whether when  he really removed the urea and reductant  whether the protein could find its way  back on its own in a test tube to the  native active form and astonishingly it  did so so this proved that a polypeptide  chain contains all of the information  for folding into the unique native  structure in its primary amino acid  sequence structure he also presumed that  in fact this native state would lie at  an energetic minimum and so this  astonishing experiment led people to  believe that perhaps inside cells  proteins also fold spontaneously but  increasingly there were instances where  that didn't really happen both in a test  tube where many proteins could also not  be spontaneously refolded as in the  antonsen experiment with ribonuclease  but inside cells when the biotech  industry began to express mammalian  proteins in e.coli for example they  often saw these terrible looking  aggregates with no biological activity  and failed to recover their favorite  proteins in a native active form in this  again one saw as in the test tube a miss  folded aggregated inclusion body as it  were and so this suggested that  something more might be required in the  cell to  deal with these so-called what would be  kinetic problems of folding where  proteins simply get it wrong and go off  pathway and miss fold and aggregate and  so we never thought we would have  anything to do with this but we were  busy in the late 80s studying  mitochondrial protein import so it turns  out that most of the proteins of  mitochondria are encoded in the nuclear  genome the messages for these proteins  are then translated on cytosolic poly  gravis ohms and an n-terminal targeting  peptide in these proteins directs them  to mitochondria and they're then  imported through the mitochondrial  membranes and that targeting peptide is  then clipped and the mature sized  protein then has to fold into its  biologically active form well an  important experiment was carried out in  1986 by Gottfried Schatz and his  co-workers that showed that an unfolded  that a protein had to be unfolded in  order to transit the mitochondrial  membranes  he used dihydrofolate reductase as a  substrate in these experiments showing  that if you lock dhfr into its native  state by adding the ligand methotrexate  the protein couldn't go through the  membranes whereas if you now removed  methotrexate or denatured the protein it  would go through the membranes so the  question that arose to us as we were  studying mitochondrial protein import  was do proteins upon reaching the  mitochondrial matrix space refold  spontaneously to their active form as  might be suggested by the ants in  experiments or could there be a  machinery that was required to assist  the protein to reach its native active  form and so we were in a position to  actually test this because we had a bank  of temperature sensitive lethal yeast  mutants that contained a galactose  regulated version of a mitochondrial  protein ornithine transcarbamylase a  human protein  that we had shown in yeast already could  be expressed and would go to faithfully  to its mitochondria have it signal  peptide cleaved and become biologically  active and make OTC enzymatic activity  and so our screen was basically to start  with temperature sensitive lethal yeast  mutants the idea being from Schatz and  others in the field that if you can't  make new mitochondrial proteins in new  mitochondria the cell basically stops  its growth and ultimately is it's a  lethal so we started with the Bank of  lethal mutants program to express  mitochondrial OTC behind a gal operon  promoter and we proceeded to test each  mutant individually for the ability to  make OTC activity after shift to  non-permissive temperature and shift  into galactose to turn on OTC expression  under non-permissive conditions we then  analyzed OTC activity if it wasn't  present we would then western blot the  particular mutant after shift to 37  degrees an induction of OTC to see what  the fate of the OTC subunit was was at  present as a mature sized protein or was  it still a precursor that hadn't been  recognized by mitochondria and so our  idea was to dissect the mitochondrial  import pathway well as we were asking  this question about refolding inside the  mitochondrial matrix we knew what we  would be looking for in the form of a  mutant that affects refolding in the  mitochondrial matrix space we would have  mature sized OTC subunits because their  signal peptide would be cleaved and  they'd be inside the matrix space but we  would have no enzymatic activity because  they would have failed to correctly fold  and in the case of OTC trimer eyes into  the active form of the enzyme so we  started to look through our bank of  mutants and to our astonishment almost  right away having asked this question we  found a mutant in which exactly that  happens so OTC was our initial reporter  enzyme and what we found was that OTC  subunits entered they reached their  mature size and then there was no  biological activity at  all so this astonished us it seemed like  heresy to have a result like this so we  thought AHA we'd better start to look at  endogenous yeast proteins and see what  is happening to them so we looked at  this particular protein the f1 beta  subunit ATPase of the f1 ATPase and we  found that in fact in similarly this  beta subunit had failed to assemble into  the this stock like structure and the  subunits themselves look like they had  Mis folded in the mitochondrial matrix  space we were in a bit of a quandary  because again it seemed like heresy and  we couldn't really feel comfortable  about the data at hand and we were sort  of pondering what proteins to look at  next  when thankfully the phone rang and it  was over Cardinal and Walter Newport  calling from Munich to say why don't we  understand that you guys have some  mutants that of yeast that affect  mitochondrial protein import and we  would love to get together with you and  perhaps help on some of the biochemistry  of those mutants if you'd have some  interest so at that point in history my  lab had four people in it and we weren't  very well versed at fractionating yeast  mitochondria whereas these were world's  experts so we were just delighted to  interact and I went to Munich the next  week and presented some of the better  described mutants that we had that  affected mitochondrial signal peptide  processing but I also at the end of my  talk described this particular mutant  and Walter and Ulrich were somewhat  worried that what was happening in this  mutant was that proteins were coming in  through this contact site but weren't  getting entirely into the mitochondrial  matrix that they were poking their  n-terminal signal peptides in and the  signals were being cleaved but then the  proteins weren't entirely entering and  of course they wouldn't fold if they  couldn't enter through  membranes and so I said well I'd be  absolutely delighted to send you the  mutant and you can make mitochondria I  am sure from this mutant very  effectively and analyzed to see what's  going on and and in fact as soon as I  got back I sent the mutant to Munich and  two weeks later  Ulrich called up to say excitedly you're  right the proteins are entirely in the  mitochondrial matrix I can add exogenous  protease and it doesn't touch them so it  seems like they have gone into the  matrix space and they have failed to  fold correctly so this was a really  wonderful time where there was a lot of  back and forth with Ulrich and I  crossing the Atlantic to visit each  other and discuss the phenotype of this  mutant this is us together at his  parents house here's his lovely wife  Monica who is a biochemist herself and  has worked some on the Gro al system as  well in recent times the chaperonin of  bacteria and maybe this is her telling  us how this is all going to work out but  in any case we carried out together a  set of experiments on a number of  mitochondrial matrix proteins to see  what was happening to them and one of  the most exciting results came with this  particular protein the risky iron-sulfur  protein so this is a protein that is  imported to the matrix space and has to  be cleaved twice as a monomer before it  enters the inner mitochondrial membrane  and when we looked at this iron sulfur  protein we found it in either a  completely on Khalif state or in a once  cleaved state as if the protein had not  folded correctly and had not undergone  the proper cleavage that enables it's  correct biogenesis so this was the first  of a monomeric protein where we saw  effects in this particular mutant we at  this point we rescued the mutant with a  yeast library so we put on a bank of  yeast clones and the gene the  rescued was sequenced by Ming Chang  during I believe it was summer 1988 and  at this point we knew that there was a  modesty heat inducible protein in inside  mitochondria they've been identified the  year before by Richard Hallberg working  in Iowa in tetrahymena and we had a  feeling that Hallberg was analyzing the  sequence of the corresponding homolog in  yeast with a view to doing further  experiments in that system so on a  Saturday afternoon in that summer Ming  Chang called up Richard Hallberg with  two men to see whether her sequence of  this gene would match the sequence that  he had of the homolog he was studying  well I had gone to JFK Airport to pick  up old rush because the two of us were  going to a Gordon conference on  mitochondria the next day and my son  came with us and it was many hours but  we got back to the to our little beach  house at around 6 o'clock in the evening  and there's Ming Chang and the rest of  the lab standing in our front yard and  Ming's just jumping up and down holding  a sequencing gel saying it's a dead  match it's a dead match and we said well  what's the dead match she said I called  up Hallberg our protein corresponds to  this ring assembly then he's studying  and so that two and two came together in  a really amazing moment and we had a  wonderful evening of celebration but  basically what it amounted to was what  we then called heat shock protein 60  collectively with Richard and and Ulrich  and Walter and everybody this modestly  heat inducible protein was actually  assisting the folding of all these  proteins being imported into the  mitochondrial matrix and in fact however  HSP 60 is essential at all temperatures  so its assistance to polypeptide chain  folding is required under normal  conditions which embody often relatively  high temperatures and very high solute  concentrations in the mitochondrial  matrix  ergo this machine is essential under all  conditions  now shortly after this ulrich carried  out a beautiful experiment with yokomo  sermon where they analyzed another  monomeric protein dihydrofolate  reductase and what they could see was  that when they put a signal peptide on  dhfr to make it go into mitochondria  much as in the shots experiments letting  it go into mitochondria it became  associated with this complex with this  ring assembly that Hallberg had first  identified the HSV 60 complex as long as  ATP was relatively deprived from  mitochondria if they added ATP back to  this system they now saw a dhfr depart  from this complex and appear as a native  folded protein while the protein I  should say was associated with HSV 60 it  seemed to be non-native very protease  susceptible recognizable by antibodies  that specifically saw non-native forms  of dhfr and so these experiments really  with risky iron-sulfur protein with dhfr  convinced us that HSV 60 was some sort  of a folding machine in the  mitochondrial matrix and of course  there's some generality to this because  we saw the sequence of HSV 60 we knew  already that it was going to be  homologous and is homologous to the grow  L protein in the bacterial cytoplasm  essential in that context and had been  shown to be involved or had been  implicated early as being involved with  lambda phage at assembly now this idea  had to move to lambda phage protein  folding there was also a protein inside  of chloroplasts called the Rubisco  binding protein identified by john ellis  as being required for assembly of  Rubisco which is a co to fix a fixing  enzyme in fact the most abundant protein  in the biosphere this now had to go to  the level of thinking that Rubisco  binding protein was actually mediating  protein folding of Rubisco subunits and  finally shortly thereafter working  together or ish and I with Jonathan  Trent identified a heat shock protein in  thermophilic arca bacteria that we  called thermophilic  factor 55 another double ring assembly  that is highly induced when you take  these normally heat-loving bacteria and  take them to an even higher temperature  that turns out to look just like a  chaperonin and it also could turn over a  teepee and behave just like these other  ring assemblies and in fact when we  analyze this subunit we found a homology  to a protein in the eukaryotic cytosol  called TCP one or CCT for short this  being the eukaryotic Sam ember of the  eukaryotic cytosolic chaperone and one  subunit of the eight subunit rings that  comprised that chaperon an essential for  folding actin tubulin bethe propeller  proteins and many other proteins so the  generality of these ring assemblies and  mediating protein folding to the native  state seem to be getting established  and so finally mechanistically twenty  years later we understand at least how  the bacterial chaperonin system works in  pretty good detail now with much  biochemistry x-ray crystallography NMR  studies and a lot of e/m conducted on  this particular machinery the Gro yell  machine and it's cooperating component  grow yes and the bottom line in terms of  mechanism is that an open ring of Gro  yell with all this hydrophobic surface  specifically binds non-native proteins  through their exposure of hydrophobic  surface that will be buried to the  interior in the native state thus Gro  yell specifically recognizes non-native  proteins and once it's bound to protein  when grow yes now encapsulate s' the  same ring that's bound by poly pipe by  poly peptide the polypeptide is released  into this hydrophilic folding chamber  all this blue here signifies  electrostatic residues and the  polypeptide now folds in isolation in a  sequestered environment where it simply  can't aggregate with any other protein  and it basically uses an FinCEN's  principles its primary structure to find  its way to the native state without the  fear of aggregation or going off pathway  and being lost  so in general molecular chaperones if  one wants to talk about all the classes  of these components they basically  recognize exposed hydrophobic surfaces  that are proffered when a protein miss  folds or during biogenesis and the idea  of the chaperone is to bind these  surfaces and prevent them from associate  self associating with each other in  multi molecular aggregates the idea of  all of the chaperones is to prevent  aggregation by binding these surfaces in  various geometric contexts the  remarkable thing about the chaperonins  however that's really unique is that  they use nucleotide to release the  polypeptide into a sequestered folding  chamber where the polypeptide really has  no chance of aggregation and has a  chance to fold using its own primary  structure to the native state one of  nature's really most beautiful machines  used for this final step of information  transfer so thanks very much for  listening to this lecture I hope you  enjoyed it thank you

